Revision history for Perl extension PerCUDA

{{$NEXT}}

    - Rebuild of the 2013 prototype for Perl 5.40+ and CUDA 12+
    - Translate kernels written in Perl to CUDA C++ and compile them with
      NVRTC through CUDA::DriverAPI; cache compiled kernels on disk and
      recompile a broken cache entry
    - Run kernels on the GPU or in the perl interpreter with the same results
      (perl_mode => 'compat'), or faster and slightly different ('fast')
    - Add precision => 'single' | 'double' for both engines
    - Pass scalars by value, coalesce memory access, reuse device buffers and
      the CUDA context, convert arrays in C (CUDA::DriverAPI write_array/read_array)
    - Add check => 1, which reports out-of-range array indices on both
      engines with the array, dimension, index, kernel line and thread
    - Shorter interface: run(name => value, ...), PerCUDA->context for
      options and arrays shared by kernels, constant => { NAME => number },
      size => [ ... ] or an int scalar name for an explicit grid, PerCUDA::runner
      for the runner of a :GPU sub
    - Add kernel subs: source => \&name or sub (...) { ... } (the signature
      orders the arguments), and with use PerCUDA,
      sub NAME :GPU(types, options) (signature) { ... } runs as a kernel when
      called
    - Incompatible: kernels read the thread index with
      my ($i, $j) = thread_index(); and the grid size with
      my ($ni, $nj) = grid_size(); (any names) instead of the implicit
      $x, $y, $z and $size_x, $size_y, $size_z, and the number of variables
      gives the dimensions of the grid (the dim option is gone)
    - Incompatible: the retval option is now called returns; without it,
      run now returns (and updates in place) every array the kernel writes;
      pass returns => [] for the old behavior
    - PerCUDA::configure(...) and use PerCUDA { ... } set the
      options of a package's :GPU subs at run time; PerCUDA::array makes
      device arrays on that configuration; configure, array and runner are
      functions that can be imported (use PerCUDA qw(configure array runner));
      use PerCUDA () leaves the caller's package alone
    - Kernel subs are the documented way to write kernels; string kernels
      remain for perl -e, string eval and generated kernels
    - Kernel subs need no types: parameters without one default to float
      (double with precision => 'double'), or int where an integer is needed;
      $runner->arguments lists them; array(...) without a type is a float
      (double with precision => 'double', or the type of an ndarray), and
      new(source => \&gpu_sub) takes the types and options of its attribute
    - :GPU(types, { options }): the types of every parameter (or none),
      then a hash of options of new written as for new (evaluated as Perl),
      including constant => { ... } and size => [ ... ]; kernels take
      barewords as constants, and kernel subs see use constant of their
      package
    - Types are written int, float and double (i, f and d still work);
      incompatible: PerCUDA::Array->type returns the name
    - A result ndarray of another type than its argument receives the values
      converted, instead of making run croak
    - The engine defaults to $ENV{PERCUDA_ENGINE} when set
    - Incompatible: without size, the grid is the shape of the first array
      passed non-empty, preferring arrays the kernel reads (was: the first
      array argument)
    - Add research samples to eg/ (heat equation, Poisson CG, Gray-Scott,
      N-body, Ising model, Black-Scholes, Monte Carlo option, k-means), each
      checked against a known answer, with eg/README.md
    - Add reductions of device arrays (sum, min, max, argmin, argmax) in a
      fixed order, identical on both engines
    - Add random($seed, ...), a counter-based generator identical on both
      engines; add eg/pi.pl (Monte Carlo)
    - Add exp, log, sin, cos, atan2, floor, ceil and ** with any exponent
    - Replace perl_mode with mode => 'fast' | 'compat' | 'exact'; exact uses
      PerCUDA::Math on both engines for bit-identical math functions
    - sqrt and log of out-of-domain values give nan/-inf on the perl engine
      instead of dying, as on the GPU
    - Add $runner->array (PerCUDA::Array): arrays kept with the engine
      between runs, without transfers
    - Add author/bench-life.pl (Game of Life: perl, nested, ndarray, device)
    - Add perl_jobs to run the perl engine in several processes
    - Skip uploading outputs the kernel overwrites entirely, and upload an
      array passed for two read-only arguments once
    - Accept PDL ndarrays for array arguments (copied without conversion on
      the GPU engine); PDL stays optional
    - Add examples (eg/), author tests, perltidy, Perl::Critic, gitleaks,
      pre-commit hooks and GitHub Actions
